D. Exception handling design
Each Callable handles expected file-related failures independently.

For example:
try {
    // Read and validate score file
    // Calculate result
} catch (IOException e) {
    // Record file reading failure
} catch (NumberFormatException e) {
    // Record invalid score format
} catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
    // Record invalid marks
}


This prevents an expected failure in one file from terminating the entire processing run.

The main thread also handles ExecutionException when retrieving a Future, because an unexpected exception may escape from a task.

Stage 3 – Implement and Measure (Student B) | 3 Marks
A. Complete Java implementation
The following implementation assumes each score file contains one line in this format:
Student101,85


The first value is the student ID, and the second value is the marks out of 100. A missing file, malformed entry or invalid mark is recorded as a failure.

import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

class ProcessingResult {
    String studentId;
    int marks;
    boolean success;
    String message;

    ProcessingResult(String studentId, int marks,
                     boolean success, String message) {
        this.studentId = studentId;
        this.marks = marks;
        this.success = success;
        this.message = message;
    }
}

class ScoreFileTask implements Callable<ProcessingResult> {
    private final Path file;

    ScoreFileTask(Path file) {
        this.file = file;
    }

    public ProcessingResult call() {
        try (BufferedReader br = Files.newBufferedReader(file)) {
            String line = br.readLine();

            if (line == null || line.trim().isEmpty()) {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Empty file");
            }

            String[] data = line.split(",");

            if (data.length != 2 || data[0].trim().isEmpty()) {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid file format");
            }

            String studentId = data[0].trim();
            int marks = Integer.parseInt(data[1].trim());

            if (marks < 0 || marks > 100) {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid marks");
            }

            String result = marks >= 50 ? "PASS" : "FAIL";

            return new ProcessingResult(
                studentId, marks, true, result
            );

        } catch (IOException | NumberFormatException |
                 IllegalArgumentException e) {

            return new ProcessingResult(
                file.getFileName().toString(),
                0, false, e.getMessage()
            );
        }
    }
}

public class ResultProcessor {

    public static void process(Path folder, int fileCount,
                               int poolSize) throws InterruptedException {

        ExecutorService executor =
                Executors.newFixedThreadPool(poolSize);

        ConcurrentLinkedQueue<ProcessingResult> results =
                new ConcurrentLinkedQueue<>();

        List<Future<ProcessingResult>> futures =
                new ArrayList<>();

        long start = System.nanoTime();

        try {
            // Submit one task for every expected file
            for (int i = 1; i <= fileCount; i++) {
                Path file = folder.resolve(
                    String.format("student_%04d.txt", i)
                );

                futures.add(
                    executor.submit(new ScoreFileTask(file))
                );
            }

            // Collect all task results
            for (Future<ProcessingResult> future : futures) {
                try {
                    results.add(future.get());

                } catch (ExecutionException e) {
                    results.add(new ProcessingResult(
                        "Unknown file", 0, false,
                        "Unexpected task error: " + e.getCause()
                    ));
                }
            }

        } finally {
            executor.shutdown();
        }

        executor.awaitTermination(1, TimeUnit.HOURS);

        long end = System.nanoTime();

        int success = 0;
        int failure = 0;

        for (ProcessingResult r : results) {
            if (r.success) {
                success++;
            } else {
                failure++;
                System.out.println(
                    "Failed: " + r.studentId + " - " + r.message
                );
            }
        }

        System.out.println("\nThread pool size: " + poolSize);
        System.out.println("Successful files: " + success);
        System.out.println("Failed files: " + failure);
        System.out.println("Total files: " + (success + failure));
        System.out.printf("Time taken: %.2f ms%n",
                (end - start) / 1_000_000.0);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args)
            throws InterruptedException {

        Path folder = Paths.get("scores");
        int fileCount = 10000;

        int[] poolSizes = {1, 2, 4, 8};

        for (int poolSize : poolSizes) {
            process(folder, fileCount, poolSize);
        }
    }
}



Important: Create a folder named scores and place the expected files inside it. The program expects names such as student_0001.txt, student_0002.txt, and so on. Leave some files missing or put invalid content in some files to test failure handling.

The example assumes Java 8 or later.
B. Important correction to the exception-handling code
In the implementation above, NumberFormatException is a subclass of IllegalArgumentException. Java does not allow both in the same multi-catch statement.

Use this corrected catch block in ScoreFileTask:
} catch (IOException | IllegalArgumentException e) {
    return new ProcessingResult(
        file.getFileName().toString(),
        0, false, e.getMessage()
    );
}


This handles file-reading errors, invalid number formats and validation errors.
